86413295 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088155 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0207d956-c871-5c34-8c83-998c0d45f1b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413275} {:answer/id #uuid "240b2653-be6a-543b-87fa-bc567dfdb80c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413277} {:answer/id #uuid "4b10917c-7b50-5beb-9269-5fa579194a74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413282} {:answer/id #uuid "b113d288-13d0-5627-8a97-e292566d6fa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & 48 \\\\ 8 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413284} {:answer/id #uuid "b29172ef-23dc-5743-a67c-26d08a2db9a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413285}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413285} :answer-field/id #uuid "1235c3ee-01a6-5118-9232-ffc94935dca5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413286}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7193e739-601b-592a-8f0d-83363b6d9331" :question/math-academy-id "q-38046" :question/problem "A  linear transformation $T$ represents a dilation. Given that $v=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $T(v)=[\\begin{aligned}48 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],$ what is the standard matrix of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "We can write $T(v)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(v) & =[\\begin{aligned}48 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =6\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =6v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $T(v)=6v,$ and so the linear transformation $T$ is a dilation with scale factor $k=6.$\n\nThe matrix that represents a dilation of scale factor $k$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}k & 0 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $T$ is\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9df23eb1-7334-5ee3-99e0-0928ef712e13" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413299}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413296 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290295 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f551863-1003-54d9-9229-d0f68c1831c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288140} {:answer/id #uuid "193ff9f7-6fba-5503-bb9c-3a2cb6e090f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288193} {:answer/id #uuid "69040895-fcef-58f4-84e4-888b5f9e6164" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288769} {:answer/id #uuid "7d797220-d751-549e-88aa-40a95669f0fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288883} {:answer/id #uuid "b67ec2f9-c5e5-5ac3-b007-ad7078c475e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289240}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288883} :answer-field/id #uuid "69f05d9b-7655-5c8a-bc09-cdbafc2ab5af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289960}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2ae1f3c1-6a60-5c1d-a29f-77af9f4d031b" :question/math-academy-id "q-212458" :question/problem "Let $a=⟨-5,-3,1⟩$ and $b=⟨x,-2,-3⟩.$ Given that the projection of $a$ onto $b$ is the zero vector, find the value of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $a$ is a nonzero vector, its projection onto another nonzero vector $b$ equals the zero vector only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\na⊥b\\,⟹\\,a\\cdot b=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =-5\\cdot x+(-3)\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =3-5x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =0 \\\\ 3-5x & =0 \\\\ -5x & =-3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a93ae252-6828-598e-a0ad-d2413fcaf43a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413293}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413297 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413289 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2650b1f4-0b37-597f-9c58-6fa55b3f1254" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413278}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413278} :answer-field/id #uuid "448b4c89-4022-50f5-af1b-27d8086bba6c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413287}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "23470b63-10f9-5736-89ca-b9c89f21d360" :question/math-academy-id "q-347226" :question/problem "The fifth-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\n{P}_{5}(x)=6+12(x-1)+9(x-1)^{2}+4(x-1)^{3}+\\frac{5}{4}(x-1)^{4}+\\frac{3}{10}(x-1)^{5}.\n$$\n\nWhat is ${f}^{(5)}(1)?$\n\n${f}^{(5)}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The coefficient of $(x-1)^{5}$ is given by $\\frac{{f}^{(5)}(1)}{5!}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{f}^{(5)}(1)}{5!} & =\\frac{3}{10} \\\\ \\frac{{f}^{(5)}(1)}{120} & =\\frac{3}{10} \\\\ {f}^{(5)}(1) & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "cbe80e64-6cb2-5624-93e4-aec8efbbce68" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413292}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413298 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297271 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0d9acb3-2434-56be-aa4c-a64c219f0e19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296551}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296551} :answer-field/id #uuid "12f4d372-a091-54bb-b1c9-2dc96c45bdc3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296674}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a9813cef-279a-5fd4-8e50-a7601280fe38" :question/math-academy-id "q-302240" :question/problem "What is the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ for $x<0$ with $f(x)=\\frac{|8x-7|}{-5x+2}?$\n\nThe horizontal asymptote is $y=$\n\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "For large negative values of $x,$ we have $|8x-7|=-(8x-7).$ Consequently, for $x\\to -\\infty ,$ we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{|8x-7|}{-5x+2}={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-(8x-7)}{-5x+2}.\n$$\n\nThe horizontal asymptote for $x<0$ is $y={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-(8x-7)}{-5x+2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-(\\frac{8x}{x}-\\frac{7}{x})}{(\\frac{-5x}{x}+\\frac{2}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-(8-\\frac{7}{x})}{(-5+\\frac{2}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(8-0)}{-5+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=\\frac{8}{5}.$"} :step/id #uuid "edaa7a81-43c4-5fba-ba12-3da5d171195f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413297}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413299 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308610 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f8fcbb2-96e8-57cf-8c64-d7dd950ff7b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-p" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306214}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306214} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8eaeee4-55d9-5263-89e5-6270bb7aa9c3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308200}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ae73a5ce-ed7c-55ec-b81b-c25a686080d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-334383" :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{|x|}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=p,$ express the value of ${\\int }_{-2}^{0}{e}^{|x|}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ in terms of $p.$\n\n${\\int }_{-2}^{0}{e}^{|x|}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x),$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x).$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)={e}^{|x|}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & ={e}^{|-x|}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x) \\\\  & ={e}^{|x|}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)) \\\\  & =-{e}^{|x|}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)={e}^{|x|}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{e}^{|x|}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the adjacent intervals rule for integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{e}^{|x|}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-2}^{0}{e}^{|x|}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{|x|}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-2}^{0}{e}^{|x|}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x+p & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-2}^{0}{e}^{|x|}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-p.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ff8eae31-cddd-545b-af34-d6fd6b1a06be" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413296}}] :activity/title "Quiz 4" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045460 :db/ident :activity.type/assessment} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413274}]}
